# Scenario 007 — CSV transformation.
#
# Anything here is a DEFAULT: a value already set in the environment wins.

# The primary route: parse and project. Point ROUTE at /transform-rich to measure
# the same parse with the rest of the CEL vocabulary layered on top.
ROUTE=/transform
READY_ROUTE=/health

TUNABLES="workers buffer pool"

# --- runtime capability -------------------------------------------------------
# This scenario's decoder is written in CEL, using functions the runtime only
# gained when the cel-go utility libraries were registered. Released 0.4.3 does not
# declare them, and a run against it would get as far as starting the target and
# then fail to build the flow.
#
# The harness asks the artifact itself — `octo eval` — rather than comparing
# version strings, because a version string cannot answer the question: a build
# from a source checkout reports the same constant as the release it branched
# from, feature or no feature.
#
# One expression, covering every library the two flows draw on. It has to compile
# AND return true, so a function that is declared but behaves differently fails the
# gate as loudly as one that is missing.
REQUIRES_CEL='["b","a","a"].distinct().sort().join(",") == "a,b" && "a,b".split(",").size() == 2 && [1,2].transformList(i, v, i > 0, v) == [2] && regex.extract("x1", "x(\\d)").orValue("") == "1" && base64.encode(bytes("x")) == "eA==" && math.round(1.5) == 2.0 && sets.contains(["a"], ["a"]) && "%.2f".format([1.5]) == "1.50" && "A".lowerAscii() == "a" && "  a  ".trim() == "a"'

# --- payload ladder -----------------------------------------------------------
# Sized in RECORDS, not bytes, so a rung here holds the same data as the rung of
# the same name in scenario 006 and the two can be compared directly. CSV is about
# half the size of the equivalent JSON, so equal bytes would mean unequal work.
#
#   PAYLOAD_RECORDS=9     ~0.6 KB CSV   (006's 1 KB JSON rung)
#   PAYLOAD_RECORDS=753    ~47 KB CSV   (006's 100 KB JSON rung)
#   PAYLOAD_RECORDS=7700  ~480 KB CSV   (006's 1 MB JSON rung)
PAYLOAD_RECORDS=9

# --- steady state -------------------------------------------------------------
# 4,000 is scenario 006's steady rate, chosen here for exactly that reason: at the
# same rung the two scenarios then offer the same load over the same records, and
# what separates them is the input format alone.
#
# It is also well inside what the flow can take. Sizing runs on m1pro-16gb against
# a source build put the default rung's closed-model ceiling near 20,000 req/s for
# /transform and near 15,000 for /transform-rich — harness calibration, not a
# result, and not from a released runtime.
STEADY_RATE=4000
STEADY_DURATION=60s
WARMUP_DURATION=10s

# --- capacity -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The peak brackets the ceiling above rather than stopping short of it, so the ramp
# actually reaches the knee. Read the top of this ramp with care: past roughly
# 15,000 req/s the generator is competing with the runtime for the same cores, so
# the closed-model ladder below is the better saturation instrument.
CAPACITY_START_RATE=200
CAPACITY_PEAK_RATE=20000
CAPACITY_STEP_DURATION=15s
CAPACITY_STEPS=10

# --- closed-model VU sweep ----------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately the same ladder as scenario 006, so the two curves can be drawn on
# one chart. See COMPARISON.md.
SCENARIO_VU_LEVELS="1 5 10 25 50 100 300"
VU_DURATION=30s

# --- k6 client sizing ---------------------------------------------------------
# Steady-state latency here is sub-millisecond, which would argue for a smaller
# number — but the pool also has to absorb the first second of a run, where a cold
# runtime is two orders of magnitude slower and a pool sized for the steady state
# starves and drops iterations. Those drops are the generator's, not the server's,
# and they are indistinguishable from saturation in the summary.
#
# 5 ms is also what scenario 006 uses, so the two scenarios offer load through an
# identically sized client — which is a precondition for comparing them at all.
EXPECTED_LATENCY_MS=5

# --- thresholds ---------------------------------------------------------------
THRESHOLD_P95_MS=50
THRESHOLD_P99_MS=120

# --- sweep grid ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Expected to find nothing, for the same reason as 006: the flow never blocks, so
# extra workers only add scheduling overhead. Recorded anyway — "tuning does not
# help here" is a result.
SCENARIO_SWEEP_WORKERS="8 32 128"
SCENARIO_SWEEP_BUFFER="64 512"
SCENARIO_SWEEP_POOL="8"

SAMPLE_INTERVAL=1.0
